## pandas

- Write with one indexer: `.loc[rows, columns] = value` by label, `.iloc` by position. Never chain two indexers in an assignment (`frame[mask]["fee"] = 0`): the value is written to a temporary copy and lost under copy-on-write.
- Assigning a whole column (`frame["fee_eur"] = frame["fee"] * rate`) is fine.
- No `inplace=True`. It rarely saves memory and it hides the data flow; rebind the name instead (`invoices = invoices.dropna(subset=["due"])`). The one exception is a measured memory fix, at top level, with a comment that says so.
- Write a pipeline as one parenthesized chain, one step per line: `assign`, filters through `.loc[lambda frame: ...]`, `rename`, and `pipe` into named functions that take a frame and return one. Use lambdas rather than `query` or `eval` strings, so linters and readers see the column names. Split a chain that outgrows a screen into named functions joined by `pipe`.
- Aggregate with named aggregation and a flat result: `groupby("carrier", as_index=False).agg(total_fee=("fee_eur", "sum"))`. Use `transform` to broadcast a group result back onto every row, and pass `observed=True` when grouping by a categorical column, so categories with no rows do not appear.

```python
monthly_fees = (
    shipments.assign(month=lambda frame: frame["shipped_at"].dt.to_period("M"))
    .loc[lambda frame: frame["status"] == "delivered"]
    .pipe(convert_to_eur, rates=eur_rates)
    .groupby(["carrier", "month"], as_index=False)
    .agg(total_fee=("fee_eur", "sum"), parcels=("shipment_id", "count"))
)
```
{% endif %}
{% if has_ml_dep("polars") %}

## polars

- Frame methods return a new frame, so rebind the name after each step. Keep the few that work in place (`insert_column`, `extend`) for frames the function created.
- Build columns with expressions inside `with_columns` and `select`, and aggregate with `group_by(...).agg(pl.col("fee_eur").sum().alias("total_fee"))`.
- For a large pipeline, start lazily (`pl.scan_parquet`, `pl.scan_csv` or `.lazy()`), chain the steps, and call `.collect()` once at the end, so the query planner sees the whole pipeline before it reads any data.
